@page
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = null;
}
<section class="hero">
  <div class="pitch">
    <h1>Pick a play. Turn it around. Every kid knows what to do.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Huddle Board is a sideline play tool for 6U to 8U flag football, or any
      division where the coach is on the field with the kids — 5-on-5, 6-on-6 or 7-on-7,
      no blocking, rusher seven yards back. The coach picks a play on a tablet and the
      kids watch it run.</p>
    <p><a class="go big" href="/app/">Open Huddle Board</a></p>
    <p class="small">Runs in the browser and <a href="#install">installs to the home screen</a>. Works with the radio off.</p>
  </div>
  <img class="shot" src="~/img/deck.png" alt="The deck: a coach's plays as cards, filtered by run, pass and situation" width="800">
</section>

<section class="points">
  <article>
    <h2>Nine shapes, and no tenth</h2>
    <p>Every job a kid runs is GO, OUT, IN, SIT, CORNER, POST, WHEEL, SWING or CARRY.
      The playbook can grow all season without a seven-year-old learning a new word.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h2>Blue and orange, never left and right</h2>
    <p>Sides are colours, because young players mix up left and right — and left and right
      flip depending on where the coach is standing.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h2>Shapes, not letters</h2>
    <p>No W, no QB on the field. A kid knows he is WIDE BLUE: a circle, a colour and a
      name he can say out loud.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h2>Built for the sideline</h2>
    <p>No network, no account, no server to sync through. Assistant coaches' tablets
      stay in step by passing one small file over Quick Share.</p>
  </article>
</section>

<section class="feature">
  <img class="shot" src="~/img/play.png" alt="A play lined up on the field, every kid marked by shape and colour" width="800">
  <div>
    <h2>Every route drawn, the ball where it ends up</h2>
    <p>A play opens on its finished picture — what a coach turns around to show. Tap
      <b>Run it</b> and it plays once from the snap: handoffs, pitches, reverses and the
      throw, with the ball in the hands of whoever actually has it.</p>
    <p>Draw your own plays at the kitchen table. The tablet checks them against the same
      rules as the shipped library before they can reach a kid.</p>
  </div>
</section>

@* Installing has to start from /app/: that page carries the manifest, so an
   install from here would only be a bookmark for the website. *@
<section class="install" id="install">
  <h2>Put it on the home screen</h2>
  <p class="lede">Installed, it opens full screen with no address bar, turns sideways
    on its own, and works with no signal at the field. Do this once per tablet, at home,
    on wifi.</p>
  <div class="steps">
    <article>
      <h3>Android tablet or phone</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>In <b>Chrome</b>, tap <a href="/app/">Open Huddle Board</a>. Install from the
          app itself, not from this page.</li>
        <li>Tap the <b>⋮</b> menu, then <b>Install app</b> or <b>Add to Home screen</b>.</li>
        <li>Open it from the new <b>Huddle Board</b> icon from now on, not from a Chrome tab.</li>
      </ol>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>iPad or iPhone</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>In <b>Safari</b>, tap <a href="/app/">Open Huddle Board</a>.</li>
        <li>Tap <b>Share</b> (the square with the arrow), then <b>Add to Home Screen</b>.</li>
        <li>Tap <b>Add</b>, and open it from the icon from now on.</li>
      </ol>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Laptop, Chrome or Edge</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <a href="/app/">Huddle Board</a>.</li>
        <li>Click the install icon at the right end of the address bar, or the menu, then
          <b>Install Huddle Board</b>.</li>
        <li>It opens in its own window and has its own icon.</li>
      </ol>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="small">Open it once more with a signal after installing, so it is ready
    offline. When a new version is out, the app offers <b>Update ready</b> on the deck. It
    never updates in the middle of a play. To remove it, press and hold the icon and
    uninstall, the same as any app. Everything you set up stays on that tablet.</p>
</section>
